INT. CANDLE-MAKER'S WORKSHOP - LONDON - NIGHT, 1666

A low timber room that smells of tallow and smoke. Cauldrons of molten wax squat on iron tripods. Rows of tapers hang from the rafters like pale stalactites. Through the shuttered window comes a dull orange glow that is not sunrise.

MASTER EZEKIEL THORNE (60s, apron stiff with wax, beard singed at the tips) stands at a vat, stirring with both hands as though his life depended on it.

His wife, MISTRESS AGNES THORNE (50s, severe, with a ring of keys at her belt) drags a chest across the floor.

Their apprentice, ALDOUS PEEL (16, thin, ink-stained fingers) crouches beside the window, peering through a crack.

Near the hearth, MERCY FAIRWEATHER (20s, a lodger, shawl pulled tight, a bird in a wicker cage clutched to her chest) huddles in silence.

And in the doorway, framed against the night, stands CAPTAIN BARNABY LOCKE (40s, soot-blackened coat, a constable's staff) breathing like a bellows.

LOCKE
The wind has turned! It comes down Pudding Lane toward you! Out, all of you, now!

EZEKIEL
(without turning)
Forty years I have poured wax in this room.

AGNES
Ezekiel.

EZEKIEL
(to himself)
Forty years, and never a fire.

AGNES
There is a fire now, you stubborn goat.

ALDOUS
Master, the glow has reached the baker's roof.

EZEKIEL
Hush, boy.

ALDOUS
It is not hushing, sir. It is coming.

Mercy's bird chirps, a small frantic sound. She presses her cheek against the cage.

MERCY
(whispering)
Hush, hush, hush.

LOCKE
There is a boat at Queenhithe. I can take four.

AGNES
We are five.

LOCKE
Then someone must find another way.

A silence. Agnes looks at her husband, at the wax, at the girl with the bird. Ezekiel finally sets down the paddle.

EZEKIEL
(to the room)
Everyone take what they can carry. Leave the rest.

AGNES
(to Ezekiel)
You will carry me, then.

EZEKIEL
I will carry the chest.

AGNES
We are leaving the chest!

EZEKIEL
(softly)
It holds the deed.

Aldous grabs a bundle of tapers. Mercy rises with her cage. Locke steps aside as they hurry past, one by one, into the street.

Ezekiel is last. He stops at the threshold and looks back at the vats, the rafters, the pale rows of unlit candles.

EZEKIEL (CONT'D)
(murmuring)
Burn bright, then. Burn bright.

He shoulders the chest and goes out. The doorway fills with orange light.
